WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.159
We have two engineered ways to hold sand together. The obvious question, if

2
00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:07.397
we run both in one crust, do the strengths simply add up?

3
00:00:08.063 --> 00:00:11.501
A sticky bio-polymer, and cement grown from carbon dioxide.

4
00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.259
Each adds its own cohesion between the grains.

5
00:00:14.359 --> 00:00:18.099
Naively you just stack them. But three real effects bend that answer.

6
00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:21.951
First, competition. Both bind the same soil calcium, but

7
00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:25.712
calcite is a high-affinity sink and out-competes the polymer.

8
00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.534
So the calcium shortfall costs gamma-P-G-A about sixty percent

9
00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:32.370
of its binding, and the cement almost nothing.

10
00:00:34.137 --> 00:00:37.067
Second, burden. One cell making both products shares

11
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.572
a single energy budget, so each titre falls

12
00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:42.370
to roughly four-fifths of what it reaches alone.

13
00:00:42.436 --> 00:00:45.447
Third, synergy, and it pushes back the other way.

14
00:00:45.547 --> 00:00:49.257
The polymer's acidic groups organise calcium and seed finer,

15
00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:51.923
tougher calcite, so a bonus is added back.

16
00:00:52.023 --> 00:00:55.586
Add the synergy, subtract the competition and the burden, and the combined

17
00:00:55.686 --> 00:00:58.506
crust still comes out tougher than either route on its own.

18
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.749
And there is a safety reason to run both.

19
00:01:00.849 --> 00:01:03.999
If a rainstorm dissolves the polymer, the insoluble calcite

20
00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.754
still holds. Two routes fail in different ways, so

21
00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:10.114
together they survive conditions that break either one alone.

